#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446241 :question/problem "Given the function  $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)^{2}$, find its directional derivative at the\npoint $A(-1,1)$ in the direction that's towards the point $B(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,4⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4x({x}^{2}+y),\\,2({x}^{2}+y)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1),2\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446242 :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ at the point $A(4,3)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,1⟩-⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}}⟨-3,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}⟨-3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(4,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(4,3) & =⟨\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(4,3) & =∇f(4,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4(-3)+3(-2)}{5\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446243 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(8,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(8,2) & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{8}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction of $-∇f(8,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(8,2)‖ & =‖-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446244 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨5,12⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(2,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}}=13 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{13}⟨5,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x,\\,-6y⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,1) & =⟨8(2),-6(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨16,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇g(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨16,-6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}⟨5,12⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{16(5)-6(12)}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{80-72}{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446245 :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)={x}^{y}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{x}^{y-1},\\,{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨(1){1}^{1-1},\\,({1}^{1})\\operatorname{ln}⁡1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $∇g(1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇\\,g(1,1)‖ & =‖⟨1,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446246 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(10-3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6x,\\,-2y,\\,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,-\\frac{1}{2},-1) & =⟨-6(1),-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right),-3(-1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-6,1,-3⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446247 :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,2,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{xy},\\,x{e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2},\\,6yz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,2,-1) & =⟨2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2},\\,2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2}+3(-1)^{2},\\,6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446572 :question/problem "![](images/5a/5ac051f640c2b4eae760eee9c567ec35ce3169384ec2305554e21c6d2d587bbe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\ne \\mathbf{0}$\n\nIII. $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n\nIV. $\\mathbf{F}$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\ne \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n![](images/80/80c67e624f960147b7980ee90700be800a414ab1e2a2e47faf7e8821ee0c0623.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}$ everywhere in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$, we say that $\\mathbf{F}$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\ne \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\ne \\mathbf{0},$ the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446573 :question/problem "![](images/18/183ea541b33b8389de46f9ee1560ed1da2b999191477d7490eb4970922bdc43c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}$\n\nII. $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\ne \\mathbf{0}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{F}$ is not irrotational\n\nIV. $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\ne \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n![](images/b1/b1520791fcb3808604758b7d8f99443020d12075d4cc8bff78659880575b6ef2.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}$ everywhere in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$, we say that $\\mathbf{F}$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- Statements III and IV are false. Since $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0},$ the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is irrotational. And, since $\\text{curl}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathbf{0},$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446574 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=(3x+2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x+2y) \\\\  & =-2x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-2,1)} & =-2(-2)-2 \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446930 :question/problem "![](images/50/5052773d05690f49969487f5ec75a1cdd15c278c1c7552dd5d811ea2e023256c.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+1+3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446931 :question/problem "![](images/07/07dd1e37f29e06051c7fe84210d41857c7c871ce577c71a86781be4ce7a0ef23.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{49}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}7\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =7(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446932 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r={4}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={4}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={4}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({4}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({4}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{4}^{2\\theta }+{4}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡4){4}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{4}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {4}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({4}^{\\pi /2}-{4}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446933 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446934 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={2(-2)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =4-2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446935 :question/problem "![](images/81/81632c931b0846520a056a872cdf327b9031fea4e3b91bf2374973fff1a2354b.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.\n$$*\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{(3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9+18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3i}\\sqrt{18+18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={6\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\theta }{2}\\right))|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =12(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =12(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447005 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Calculate $$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathrm{∇}f\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r},\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,-1,1)$ to $(2,1,0)$ in ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,-1,1)$ and $B(2,1,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,1,0)-f(0,-1,1) \\\\  & =(2(1)^{2}+{0}^{3})-(0(-1)^{2}+{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447370 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-y)$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,\\pi ) & =2(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{2}-\\pi )\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{2}-\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =0\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(0,\\pi )\\cdot ⟨x-0,\\,y-\\pi ⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,\\,y-\\pi ⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447371 :question/problem "At which point on the curve ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=xy$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =xy \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-xy & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-xy,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(-2y-x)\\,i-(2x-y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i,$ we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((-2y-x)\\,i-(2x-y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ -2y-x & =0 \\\\ x & =-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2y$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =xy \\\\ (-2y)^{2}-{y}^{2} & =(-2y)y \\\\ 4{y}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =-2{y}^{2} \\\\ 5{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $y=0,$ we get $x=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447372 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $(x-y)^{2}=2x-y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2} & =2x-y \\\\ (x-y)^{2}-2x+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}-2x+y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,0)={f}_{y}(2,0)i-{f}_{x}(2,0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)^{2}-2x+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{2}-2x+y)j \\\\  & =(-2(x-y)+1)i-(2(x-y)-2)j \\\\ t(2,0) & =(-2((2)-(0))+1)\\,i-((2((2)-(0))-2)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-3\\,i-2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447373 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=3xy+4x+6y-5{y}^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy+4x+6y-5{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy+4x+6y-5{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(3y+4)\\,i+(3x+6-10y)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,2) & =(3(2)+4)\\,i+(3(1)+6-10(2))\\,j \\\\  & =10\\,i-11\\,j \\\\  & =⟨10,-11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨10,-11⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447374 :question/problem "At which point on the curve $x{y}^{2}=3-2y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2} & =3-2y \\\\ x{y}^{2}+2y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}+2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2xy+2)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i,$ we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2xy+2)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2xy+2 & =0 \\\\ xy+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2} & =3-2y \\\\ x{\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{2} & =3-2\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =3+\\frac{2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we get $y=3,$ and the corresponding point is $(-\\frac{1}{3},3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455447 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455463 :question/problem "![](images/13/13e4c6a06a44099024ce91ef6c9ed4679af037c2e794351597a4d604bf39dc6f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n{∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/63/6371bb04d33719ee18c65a92354e84be0e9077e780eac9559dcb7ec42deb8d99.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/0b/0bd9476fa9b19f04b40fbb9cca2986ed90495aa134307606622ef91070af19a7.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455485 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1883ee3a3a99d43109bae2d2093aef35548dde9753cfb7dc19dfd77a104ec83.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n{∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/85/8595904122fac3431fc959b2fa4aa5a3062bd1bffd1a391a0861fa12f2498054.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1f31f2b6eefddedc46b8e845703904c0b4566963e64a644f7a4ab32bb7240c3.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455487 :question/problem "![](images/d0/d042734a8c7483c54795cd6ec8527b43f398cb77487d054a36b27d704f27a318.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n{∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}<0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/f8/f8268167bb50f9d03cb38a3d505fa6b40003e307b149356089f5b0dc3dd32110.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a83934aa58040b4eb2bb957d1d12caba676e306e41086480d064935eab9f1c59.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455499 :question/problem "![](images/36/36ebce64036b7661c81aa6525c407f698375544532985175cc465ddc3ff4f5a8.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n{∮}_{-C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}>0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/c4/c498ddeae8ba4325a4a1dfc0840520c4718bb50a0600970405374dde933e1b1d.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/74/740429a29b5eb4b72bbfae11e08c9ac272e72e31d395b4dad2602beb4d4ba40a.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459208 :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb2da6fbb22b19d027212685ca521f5186a831502617c2089766c5e9fe739696.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}(1+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+({x}^{2}+3xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/48/4822bfa926feccb0a12c14580ac325f4f10a0e4f815d0247b2231bf08b8c2326.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}(1+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}(1+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+({x}^{2}+3xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+3y-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459212 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(3,1,2),$ $B(0,5,0),$ $C(3,3,1),$ and $D(1,4,2).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(5j)-(3i+1j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+4j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,4,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(3i+3j+1k)-(3i+1j+2k) \\\\  & =2j-1k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(1i+4j+2k)-(3i+1j+2k) \\\\  & =-2i+3j \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(2\\cdot 0-(-1)(3))-4(0\\cdot 0-(-1)(-2))+(-2)(0\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-3(3)-4(-2)-2(4) \\\\  & =-9-8+8 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459217 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,1,1),$ $B(2,3,4),$ $C(-1,2,0),$ and $D(3,0,5).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+4k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i+2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+2j)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(3i+5k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i-j+4k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot (-1))-2((-2)\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3((-2)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1(4-1)-2(-8+2)+3(2-2) \\\\  & =3+12+0 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|15|=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459221 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨5,-2,2⟩,$ $\\mathbf{q}=⟨3,k,1⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-8,3,-4⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k & 1 \\\\ -8 & 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & k \\\\ -8 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(k\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 1)+2(3\\cdot (-4)-(-8)\\cdot 1)+2(3\\cdot 3-(-8)\\cdot k) \\\\  & =-20k-15-8+18+16k \\\\  & =-4k-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ -4k-5 & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{5}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459223 :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f56006dd06fab3bbc736c45ac6e9d65a401b88e0a9eb8a5db404fb67b89977e.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/f3/f393d0facc98fe365e2c622e24223d6045b48b490937ba6e2f25d86d7eb4810f.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)={y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2},\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-8{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-8{x}^{2}y)dx+({y}^{7}+8x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8{y}^{2}-(-8{x}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}8({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}8{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[2{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}32\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =32\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459234 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x-{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/04/0470588a034222b183694b087d417e8ead0d2fd72357e796373794d00523708f.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx-{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459235 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2xy=2x-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy & =2x-y \\\\ 2xy-2x+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy-2x+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-2x+y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-2x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i-(2y-2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $2\\,i-j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (2\\,i-j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (2\\,i-j) & =0 \\\\ ((2x+1)\\,i-(2y-2)\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i-j) & =0 \\\\ (4x+2)+(2y-2) & =0 \\\\ 4x+2y & =0 \\\\ y & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-2x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy & =2x-y \\\\ 2x(-2x) & =2x-(-2x) \\\\ -4{x}^{2} & =4x \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1\\,\\text{or}\\,x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459239 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(8,0,5),$ $B(8,-3,3),$ $C(5,2,5),$ and $D(7,-1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(8i-3j+3k)-(8i+5k) \\\\  & =-3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,-3,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(5i+2j+5k)-(8i+5k) \\\\  & =-3i+2j \\\\  & =⟨-3,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(7i-j+4k)-(8i+5k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+3((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)-2((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =9-10 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-1|=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459261 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-1,2,5⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,-4,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨0,5,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-((-4)\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 2)-2(1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 2)+5(1\\cdot 5-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =14-2+25 \\\\  & =37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|37|=37.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459263 :question/problem "![](images/18/184b50ce54b01453d5b645eef7edc186823e8ae34afaefa2527e34122c1722cc.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3},\\hspace{0.222em}\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+10)+2x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to $$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}(y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(\\operatorname{ln}({y}^{2}+10)+2x)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/93/93a1c20377ddd0731c6190712d9d6d77551124fa33001334e84f52049bb6b800.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1+2{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+10)+2x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-(1+2{y}^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 9,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(1-2\\cdot {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\int }_{0}^{9}(1-2{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459264 :question/problem "Find $t$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨9,9,-2⟩,$ $\\mathbf{q}=⟨5,6,0⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨4,t,t⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & t & t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ t & t\\end{vmatrix}-9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & t\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =9(6\\cdot t-t\\cdot 0)-9(5\\cdot t-4\\cdot 0)-2(5\\cdot t-4\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =54t-45t-10t+48 \\\\  & =48-t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =0 \\\\ 48-t & =0 \\\\ t & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=48.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459266 :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb6a49b2172b3a60676ef74a244298eff05ffc65be6feca089c4a953757f2e59.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy,\n$$ where the path $C$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d816d9153e22dd109e20e3e387f6a0028e3f6a9eaf1d284dfae8b5fdf54cfc6e.png)\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}-x{y}^{2},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\n we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\sqrt{x}-x{y}^{2})dx+(\\sqrt{{y}^{7}+3}+{x}^{2}y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-(-2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )]_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0] \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459275 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,2,4⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨3,5,-7⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,0,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-7))-2(3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-7))+4(3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =10-26-20 \\\\  & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|-36|=36.\n$$"}]}
